.TH MERKADU 1 "October 2026" "SDE" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
merkadu \- the SDE software center: find, install and remove apps and packages
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B merkadu
[\fB--page\fR \fINAME\fR] [\fB--search\fR \fITEXT\fR] [\fB--category\fR \fIID\fR]
[\fB--details\fR \fIAPPID\fR] [\fB--package\fR \fINAME\fR]
.br
.B merkadu
\fBappstream://\fIAPPID\fR
.br
.B merkadu
\fB--uninstall\fR \fIFILE\fB.desktop\fR
.br
.B merkadu
\fB--self-test\fR [\fB--expect\fR \fIREF\fR[,\fIREF\fR...]] [\fB--expect-flatpak\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB--list\fR]
[\fB--simulate\fR \fBinstall:\fIPKG\fR,\fBremove:\fIPKG\fR,\fBupdate:\fIPKG\fR]
.br
.B merkadu
\fB--version\fR | \fB--help\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBMerkadu\fR is the software center of the Selviana Desktop Environment. It
finds, installs, updates and removes software from three sources:
.TP
.B Debian
the packages of the system's APT sources, through PackageKit: the apps that
the archive's AppStream catalog (DEP-11) describes, and in \fBAll
Software\fR every package.
.TP
.B Flatpak
apps of the Flatpak remotes of the system installation (and those installed
for the user), with libflatpak. Flathub is set up when the \fBmerkadu\fR
package is installed; when it is missing, Merkadu offers to set it up.
.TP
.B Snap
the Snap Store, through snapd's REST API on \fI/run/snapd.socket\fR. Without
snapd, \fBEnable Snap Support\fR installs the \fBsnapd\fR package and then
the snapd snap; a restart (or a new login) finishes it.
.PP
One search covers every source. An app that more than one source offers is
shown once, with a badge per source; its page lets you choose the source to
install from and shows the version, size, license and isolation of each.
.PP
The header has \fBBack\fR, \fBForward\fR, \fBHome\fR, the search and
\fBRefresh\fR. The sidebar has \fBHome\fR (highlights, recommendations, new
and updated apps, popular apps and the categories), \fBInstalled\fR (grouped
by department), \fBUpdates\fR (updates of apps from Flatpak remotes and the
Snap Store; the operating system is updated by Software Updater,
\fBsde-update\fR(1)), \fBAll Software\fR, \fBHistory\fR (installations,
updates and removals of every source, by day), \fBSources\fR, \fBIn
Progress\fR (the queue of changes, with the animated SDE logo) and the
departments with their subcategories, which come from Ubuntu Software
Center, on which Merkadu is based.
.PP
\fBAll Software\fR lists every package of the repositories by Debian section
and status (installed, with an update, not installed, marked, settings
left), like Synaptic. Packages are searched by name and description, changes
are marked (installation, update, removal) and reviewed together with
everything they install, update and remove, then applied at once. Packages
the system needs (Essential, Priority required, the running kernel) and
SDE's own packages are never removed, and the page says why.
.PP
When Merkadu starts, it refreshes the software lists in the background: the
package lists with the archive's AppStream catalog and its icons (PackageKit),
and the catalogs of the Flatpak remotes. Lists refreshed less than 30 minutes
ago are kept; \fBRefresh\fR always refreshes. When there is no catalog,
Merkadu says why and how to fix it.
.PP
Installing, updating and removing ask for authorization with polkit (the SDE
authentication dialog): PackageKit for Debian packages, the Flatpak system
helper for the system installation and snapd for snaps. Merkadu itself runs
as the user and never as root.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --page " NAME"
open a page: \fBhome\fR, \fBinstalled\fR, \fBupdates\fR, \fBpackages\fR
(All Software), \fBhistory\fR, \fBprogress\fR or \fBsources\fR.
.TP
.BI --search " TEXT"
search for \fITEXT\fR.
.TP
.BI --category " ID"
open a department, such as \fBgames\fR or \fBgraphics/photography\fR.
.TP
.BI --details " APPID"
open the page of an app: its AppStream id (\fBorg.gimp.GIMP\fR), or
\fBdeb:\fIPACKAGE\fR, \fBflatpak:\fIREF\fR or \fBsnap:\fINAME\fR.
.TP
.BI --package " NAME"
open the page of a package in All Software: its description, versions,
dependencies and the packages that need it.
.TP
.BI appstream:// APPID
the same as \fB--details\fR; Merkadu handles the \fBappstream:\fR links of
app websites.
.TP
.BI --uninstall " FILE"
uninstall the app of a launcher, given the absolute path of its
\fI.desktop\fR file (the Start menu's \fBUninstall\fR). A small window shows
the app's icon and name, where it comes from (the Debian package that owns
the file, the Flatpak of \fBX-Flatpak=\fR or the snap of
\fBX-SnapInstanceName=\fR), the disk space it frees and the other packages
that are removed with it, and asks before removing it. A launcher in the
user's home that no package installed is removed on its own. Apps of SDE's
own packages (\fBsde\fR, \fBsde-desktop\fR, \fBmerkadu\fR) and packages the
system needs are not removed, and neither is an app whose removal would take
one of them away.
.TP
.B --self-test
load the catalogs and the package lists without a window and print where
they come from: whether APT downloads the AppStream catalog, how many
catalogs and icon archives there are, when the lists were refreshed, the
Flatpak remotes, how many apps have which kind of icon and how many
packages there are. \fB--expect\fR checks that apps are there with an icon
(AppStream ids, \fBdeb:\fIPACKAGE\fR, \fBflatpak:\fIAPPID\fR, alternatives
separated by \fB|\fR), \fB--expect-flatpak\fR that at least \fIN\fR Flatpak
apps are, \fB--list\fR prints every app and \fB--simulate\fR prints APT's
plan for marked changes.
.TP
.B --version
print the version and exit.
.TP
.B --help
print the usage and exit.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B Ctrl+F, Ctrl+K
search.
.TP
.B Alt+Left, Backspace, Escape
back.
.TP
.B Alt+Right
forward.
.TP
.B Alt+Home
home.
.TP
.B F5, Ctrl+R
refresh the software lists.
.TP
.B Space, +, \-
in All Software: mark the most likely change, mark for installation (or
update), mark for removal.
.TP
.B F1
about Merkadu.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Q
quit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
With \fB--uninstall\fR: \fB0\fR when the app was removed, \fB1\fR when it
could not be removed or Merkadu refused to remove it, \fB2\fR when the user
cancelled. With \fB--self-test\fR: \fB0\fR when everything expected was
found, \fB1\fR otherwise. Otherwise \fB0\fR, or \fB2\fR for a wrong command
line.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/merkadu/merkadu.conf
the defaults: the highlighted, popular and recommended apps of the Home
page, the number of new apps, the refresh at start, the order of the
sources, Flathub, whether to hide the snap folder and more protected
packages. Distributions override it with \fI/etc/merkadu/merkadu.conf\fR or
a file in \fI$XDG_CONFIG_DIRS/merkadu/\fR.
.TP
.I /usr/share/merkadu/categories.menu
the departments (Ubuntu Software Center's menu format); files in
\fI/usr/share/merkadu/menu.d/\fR and \fI/etc/merkadu/menu.d/\fR add or
replace departments.
.TP
.I /usr/share/merkadu/flathub.flatpakrepo
Flathub's repository file, used to set up Flathub.
.TP
.I /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/60merkadu
makes APT download the icons of the archive's AppStream catalog.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/merkadu/images
downloaded icons and screenshots.
.TP
.I /var/log/apt/history.log
the history of Debian packages; Flatpak's comes from \fBflatpak history\fR
and the Snap history from snapd's changes.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MERKADU_CONFIG
a configuration file used instead of the ones above.
.TP
.B MERKADU_FAKE
a directory with a fake catalog (\fIcatalog.json\fR): all three sources and
the package lists are simulated, nothing is installed or downloaded. For
tests and screenshots.
.SH NOTES
snapd keeps each user's snap data in \fI~/snap\fR. Before the first snap is
installed, Merkadu sets snapd's \fBexperimental.hidden-snap-folder\fR option,
which moves it to \fI~/.snap/data\fR; snapd cannot keep it outside the home
folder. See \fI/usr/share/doc/merkadu/MERKADU.md\fR.
